WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.592
Picture renting a flat in a city you will never visit.

2
00:00:06.592 --> 00:00:12.143
On AWS that flat is an EC2 instance, a virtual server you rent by the second.

3
00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:18.923
You leave a note for the caretaker, and the server unpacks hello-devops, a tiny web app, all by itself.

4
00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:21.440
The flat's door has a lock.

5
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.736
On AWS, the lock is a security group, a firewall wrapped around the server.

6
00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:30.349
Which brings up the question everybody has on day one.

7
00:00:30.349 --> 00:00:31.351
I can't walk in.

8
00:00:31.351 --> 00:00:33.856
So how do I get a shell on that machine?

9
00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:36.494
Many tutorials take the quick way.

10
00:00:36.494 --> 00:00:43.275
They open port 22, the door for SSH, the secure remote login, to every address on the internet.

11
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.696
You can get in now.

12
00:00:44.696 --> 00:00:47.088
So can everyone else who wants to try.

13
00:00:47.088 --> 00:00:51.243
Every stranger on the internet can walk up and rattle that handle.

14
00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:57.514
And the old key pair way adds a private key file you download once, and must never lose or leak.

15
00:00:57.514 --> 00:00:59.971
That is a front door key under the mat.

16
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.510
The lab does the opposite.

17
00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:06.852
Its security group, hello-devops-web, has no inbound rules at all.

18
00:01:06.852 --> 00:01:09.417
Outbound allows everything, by default.

19
00:01:09.633 --> 00:01:13.529
Nobody on the internet can start a connection to the server.

20
00:01:13.529 --> 00:01:14.373
You included.

21
00:01:14.373 --> 00:01:20.021
Congratulations: you have built the safest flat in the city, and even you can't get in.

22
00:01:20.633 --> 00:01:22.010
So here is the puzzle.

23
00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:26.513
With zero inbound rules, can any traffic ever come in through that door?

24
00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:31.312
It is tempting to say no, never.

25
00:01:31.312 --> 00:01:32.385
Let's test that.

26
00:01:32.385 --> 00:01:35.402
On first boot, the server downloads packages.

27
00:01:35.402 --> 00:01:39.156
That is the server calling out, and outbound is allowed.

28
00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:41.561
But a download is a conversation.

29
00:01:41.561 --> 00:01:45.351
The packages have to come back in, through the same door.

30
00:01:45.351 --> 00:01:46.149
And they do.

31
00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:47.723
The lock is stateful.

32
00:01:47.723 --> 00:01:53.859
It remembers the calls the flat made, and lets the replies back in without a rule of their own.

33
00:01:53.859 --> 00:01:57.928
Inbound rules are only about strangers starting a conversation.

34
00:01:58.133 --> 00:02:02.268
So the wrong model was: no inbound rules means nothing comes in.

35
00:02:02.268 --> 00:02:08.664
The right one: nobody can start a conversation from outside, but the flat can call anyone it likes.

36
00:02:09.467 --> 00:02:11.153
Now you can guess the trick.

37
00:02:11.153 --> 00:02:13.441
If the flat can call out, let it call.

38
00:02:13.441 --> 00:02:18.319
Put a phone inside, and have the flat dial the front desk and keep the line open.

39
00:02:18.533 --> 00:02:24.718
On AWS, the phone is a small program on the server called the SSM Agent.

40
00:02:24.718 --> 00:02:30.387
It keeps an outbound HTTPS connection open to AWS Systems Manager.

41
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.266
Then you run aws ssm start-session on your Mac.

42
00:02:34.266 --> 00:02:40.661
AWS checks your IAM permissions, the rules from last lesson about who may do what.

43
00:02:40.867 --> 00:02:47.174
If they allow it, AWS joins your terminal to the line the agent already holds open.

44
00:02:47.174 --> 00:02:50.821
You arrive as ssm-user, a user with sudo rights.

45
00:02:51.033 --> 00:02:52.352
Look at the door.

46
00:02:52.352 --> 00:02:53.592
It never opened.

47
00:02:53.592 --> 00:02:55.609
Zero inbound rules, still.

48
00:02:55.609 --> 00:02:57.780
No port, and no key to lose.

49
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.150
This is Session Manager, part of AWS Systems Manager.

50
00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:04.499
It costs nothing extra on EC2.

51
00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.368
The line needs four things.

52
00:03:07.368 --> 00:03:16.356
The agent on the server, which AWS's and Canonical's Ubuntu images, and Amazon Linux 2023, already include.

53
00:03:16.567 --> 00:03:23.507
An instance role with the AWS managed policy AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore.

54
00:03:23.733 --> 00:03:29.472
Outbound HTTPS from the server to three AWS endpoints in its region.

55
00:03:29.472 --> 00:03:33.944
A public IP and the default outbound rule cover that.

56
00:03:34.167 --> 00:03:38.717
And the Session Manager plugin, an add-on for the CLI, on your Mac.

57
00:03:39.433 --> 00:03:42.508
The same line carries more than a shell.

58
00:03:42.508 --> 00:03:50.732
The document AWS-StartPortForwardingSession makes localhost:8000 on your Mac reach port 8000 on the server.

59
00:03:50.933 --> 00:03:55.973
So you can open hello-devops in your own browser, through a door that is still locked.

60
00:03:55.973 --> 00:03:58.669
The caretaker holds the phone up to the radio.

61
00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:01.893
AWS gives you three ways in.

62
00:04:01.893 --> 00:04:05.495
Session Manager, with no open port and no key.

63
00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:19.616
EC2 Instance Connect pushes a one-time SSH key for you, but port 22 must still be open, either to AWS's Instance Connect address range or through an Instance Connect Endpoint.

64
00:04:19.833 --> 00:04:25.408
And key pairs, the old way: port 22, plus a file you must never lose or leak.

65
00:04:26.133 --> 00:04:29.747
So the moral of the story fits in three short lines.

66
00:04:29.747 --> 00:04:31.207
Open no inbound door.

67
00:04:31.207 --> 00:04:32.875
Let the server dial out.

68
00:04:32.875 --> 00:04:35.099
And let IAM decide who may join.

69
00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:36.801
A quick check.

70
00:04:36.801 --> 00:04:42.663
A teammate's security group allows port 22 from every address, so they can SSH in.

71
00:04:42.663 --> 00:04:46.380
Which change gives them a shell and closes the port?

72
00:04:49.033 --> 00:04:52.455
Use Session Manager, and delete the port 22 rule.

73
00:04:52.455 --> 00:04:56.855
The agent connects out, and IAM decides who may open a session.
